# Diagrams / Drawings

When asked for a diagram, drawing, architecture diagram, flowchart, system map, or "draw me ...":

**Standard: modern, dark-mode, exported to PDF (+ PNG for inline preview). NEVER use mermaid — Brad considers it ugly/not modern.**

**Enforced by:** `~/.claude/hooks/guard_file_write_rules.py`

That line is not decoration. This rule enforced nothing until 2026-08-30, and
in the gap the ops-dashboard shipped a mermaid architecture graph plus a
mermaid CDN script on every page load. The hook blocks a write that introduces
a mermaid fence, the CDN script, `mermaid.initialize`/`run`, or a
`<pre class="mermaid">` target. Writing *about* mermaid in a comment is
deliberately still allowed. Tests: `~/.claude/hooks/test_guard_file_write_rules.py`.

**It covers Bash as well as Write/Edit, since 2026-09-13 — and it did not
before.** The hook was registered `Write|Edit`, while auto mode and bypass mode
both instruct: *"make file changes with sed, heredocs, or short scripts, rather
than using the dedicated Read, Edit, or Write tools."* In either of those modes
the sentence above was **false** — a heredoc carrying a mermaid fence wrote
cleanly with the guard silent, verified that day. Naming a mechanism that
cannot fire scores worse than naming none, because it reads as covered. If you
add a rule whose mechanism is a `Write|Edit` hook, check its matcher covers
`Bash`, or the rule is decorative in the mode you are most likely running in.

Widening it surfaced a second bug: the guard had never been able to edit **this
file**, because the paragraph above quotes the render target it bans. A
backticked span in a markdown file now counts as prose. Only markdown — a
JavaScript template literal uses the same character, and there the target is
real.

For a web page, render the D2 to SVG at build time and embed it rather than
shipping a diagram library to the browser — see
[`ops-dashboard/scripts/render_diagrams.sh`](../../Projects/ops-dashboard/scripts/render_diagrams.sh).

## Tool choice
- **Default → D2** (`d2`): text → polished dark diagram, native PDF+PNG.
- **Icon-rich cloud/infra → mingrammer `diagrams`** (Python; interpreter `~/.diagram-tools/venv/bin/python`, needs Graphviz `dot`).
- **Fully bespoke visual → hand-authored SVG → `rsvg-convert`**.

## Output convention
Write into the project's `diagrams/` folder. Produce `<name>.pdf` (primary, dark) + `<name>.png` (inline preview) + keep the source (`.d2`/`.py`/`.svg`). In markdown: embed PNG, link PDF.

## Commands
```bash
# D2 (default) — dark themes: 200 Dark Mauve, 201 Dark Flagship Terrastruct
d2 --theme 200 --pad 40 diagrams/<name>.d2 diagrams/<name>.pdf
d2 --theme 200 --pad 40 diagrams/<name>.d2 diagrams/<name>.png

# mingrammer diagrams — set graph_attr bgcolor "#0b0f17" + light fontcolor for dark mode
~/.diagram-tools/venv/bin/python diagrams/<name>.py   # Diagram(..., outformat=["pdf","png"])

# bespoke SVG → PDF/PNG
rsvg-convert -f pdf -o diagrams/<name>.pdf diagrams/<name>.svg
rsvg-convert -f png -z 2 -o diagrams/<name>.png diagrams/<name>.svg
```

Claude Code: a richer `/diagram` skill exists at `~/.claude/skills/diagram/`. Always read the rendered PNG back to self-check (overlaps, legibility, dark mode) before reporting done.


# prompt-caching

# Prompt Caching — Mandatory for All Anthropic API Code

Anthropic flagged the StaveandSpirit org for a low cache hit rate (~33% potential API-spend savings, 2026-07-13). Every session that writes or touches Anthropic API code applies these rules. Source: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching

## Core rule

Any `messages.create` / `messages.stream` call that reuses a system prompt, tool list, or document context across calls MUST set a cache breakpoint. No exceptions for new code; existing code gets it whenever the file is being touched anyway (surgical-change rule still applies — don't bulk-sweep stable repos unprompted).

## How (Python — the default on this machine)

Use the bauto helpers, not hand-rolled `cache_control` dicts:

```python
from bauto import anthropic_client
client = anthropic_client.make_client("<service>", "anthropic_api_key")
resp = client.messages.create(
    model="claude-opus-4-8",
    system=anthropic_client.cached_system(BIG_STABLE_PROMPT),   # breakpoint on last block
    tools=anthropic_client.cache_tools(MY_TOOLS),               # caches tools prefix too
    messages=[...],
)
```

- Loops/daemons where reuse spans >5 min (launchd jobs, Helm, pipelines): pass `ttl="1h"` — the default 5-minute cache silently expires mid-loop and you pay full price again. 1h writes cost 2× (vs 1.25× for 5m), so it needs ≥3 reads to pay off.
- Non-bauto projects (Node, raw HTTP): put `"cache_control": {"type": "ephemeral"}` on the last block of the stable prefix (`system` blocks array or last tool definition).

## Design rules (these matter more than the marker)

Caching is a **prefix match** — one changed byte invalidates everything after it. Render order: `tools` → `system` → `messages`.

1. **Freeze the system prompt.** Never interpolate `datetime.now()`, run IDs, UUIDs, user names, or mode flags into `system`. Dynamic context goes into the user message, after the breakpoint.
2. **Deterministic serialization.** `json.dumps(..., sort_keys=True)`; never iterate a `set` into the prompt. Sort tool lists by name; don't vary the tool set per request.
3. **Stable-first ordering.** never-changes → per-session → per-turn → per-request, in that order. Breakpoint at the stability boundary, not the end of the whole prompt.
4. **Multi-turn agent loops:** also mark the last content block of the newest turn so the conversation prefix accrues incrementally.
5. **Don't cache one-shot calls** or prompts that differ from byte 0 — it only pays the write premium.
6. **Minimum cacheable prefix** 512–4096 tokens depending on model (exact per-model values below — they are *not* monotonic across generations) — short prompts silently don't cache (no error, `cache_creation_input_tokens: 0`).

## Verify (do this after adding caching)

Check `resp.usage`: `cache_read_input_tokens > 0` on the second identical-prefix call = working. Zero across repeated calls = a silent invalidator (timestamp/UUID/unsorted JSON/varying tools) — diff the rendered prompt bytes between two requests. `bauto.pricing` / `ops_metrics.log_anthropic_response` already record the cache token classes, so the ops-dashboard Cost & Usage view shows the hit rate.

## Sweep status (2026-07-13, mini) — the part worth remembering

Wired already: helm router, YT `shot_list_gen` + `script_gen`, contract-analysis,
receipts-vault. The code carries the markers — don't re-audit *those files* on a whim.

**But that is not a licence to stop looking.** A point-in-time sweep decays: spend
composition drifts, new consumers appear that the sweep never saw, and a model swap can
silently drop a prompt below its cacheable minimum. That is exactly what happened between
2026-07-13 and 2026-08-25 — brad-kb did not exist at sweep time and became 63% of direct
API spend, and Anthropic's mailing list ended up being the monitor.

**Standing verification (do this instead of re-auditing files):** the check is on *new or
changed (api_key, model) pairs*, not on already-swept code. A pair with >1M uncached
tokens/30d and zero cache reads for 7+ days is the signal. Full audit and monitor design:
[`ops-docs/audits/anthropic-cache-efficiency-2026-08-25.md`](../../Projects/ops-docs/audits/anthropic-cache-efficiency-2026-08-25.md).

**A 0% hit rate is often correct** — a once-daily job (cache TTL maxes at 1h), a one-shot
call, or a prompt under the model's minimum prefix. Never "fix" one without checking
`usage.cache_read_input_tokens` first. Minimums are **not monotonic** across generations:
512 (Opus 5), 1024 (Sonnet 5 / Sonnet 4.6 / Opus 4.8), 2048 (Opus 4.7), 4096 (Haiku 4.5,
Opus 4.6). Dropping below is a silent no-op, never an error.

**Caching is not the only lever, and often not the biggest.** It prices input only — on
the workload audited 2026-08-25, output was 53% of spend. Check model choice and the Batch
API (50%, and it halves output too) before reaching for `cache_control`.

**Evaluated and deliberately SKIPPED — do not re-attempt unless the prompt shape
changes:** `helm/app/llm/vision.py`, `plainspoke*/llm.py` + `research/*`,
`CSMYS_Automation/src/parser.py`, `HotTub/backend/vision.py`, and the
once-per-run YT stages (visual_qa, thumbnail_judge, comment_responder,
script_validator). All are one-shot and/or below the model's minimum cacheable
prefix, so a marker is a silent no-op or pure write premium.

Open idea, not wired: YT stages all embed the same ~8K-token script in different
prompts. Putting the script FIRST and instructions after would make it a shared
cacheable prefix across stages within a run. Invasive — revisit only if YT input
spend becomes a concern.


